-- Q3946: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 402(b) and Sec. 83(a), a secular trust protects assets from the employer''s creditors, which creates an immediate economic benefit to the executive. Because the assets are secured, the executive is taxed when contributions vest. In contrast, a rabbi trust under Rev. Proc. 92-64 keeps assets subject to creditors, deferring taxation until distribution.
Wrong (A): Secular trust contributions trigger taxation to the executive when vested, not a deduction-only benefit. The employer may deduct, but the executive is also taxed.
Wrong (B): There is a significant tax difference. Rabbi trusts defer taxation; secular trusts trigger immediate taxation upon vesting due to creditor protection.
Wrong (C): Secular trust distributions are ordinary income, not capital gains. The income character follows Sec. 83(a) compensation rules.$EXPL$
