-- Q3949: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): By making a Section 83(b) election, the founder includes $50,000 (100,000 × $0.50) as ordinary income immediately at grant. All future appreciation above $0.50 per share is taxed as capital gain, qualifying as long-term if held more than one year. For a startup expecting significant growth, this converts potential ordinary income at vesting into lower-taxed capital gains.
Wrong (A): The founder does not avoid all income tax. The election requires immediate recognition of $50,000 in ordinary income at the time of grant.
Wrong (C): A Sec. 83(b) election accelerates taxation to the grant date rather than deferring it. Deferral occurs when no election is made.
Wrong (D): The shares are compensation, not a business expense. The employee cannot deduct the value of the restricted stock received.$EXPL$
